WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.440
This week, NASA's work in low-Earth orbit took center stage.

00:03.440 --> 00:07.680
From crew rotations to spacecraft development, here's what's new in your NASA Minute.

00:07.680 --> 00:10.400
Crew 13 is officially aboard station.

00:10.400 --> 00:14.960
On Thursday morning, NASA and SpaceX launched the next crew to the International Space Station

00:14.960 --> 00:17.200
from Cape Canaveral Space Force Station.

00:17.200 --> 00:20.400
Carrying NASA astronauts Jessica Watkins and Luke Delaney,

00:20.400 --> 00:26.480
Canadian Space Agency astronaut Joshua Kutrick and Roscosmos Cosmonaut Sergei Tetriotnikov.

00:26.480 --> 00:30.240
Just hours later, there are Dragon spacecraft docked with the Orbiting Laboratory,

00:30.240 --> 00:33.600
with a crew joined Expedition 75 to continue the science,

00:33.600 --> 00:37.600
maintenance and technology demonstrations happening every day in low-Earth orbit.

00:38.320 --> 00:41.200
And the next crew rotation is already taking shape.

00:41.200 --> 00:45.440
Last week, NASA announced that NASA astronauts Caleb Barron and Chris Birch,

00:45.440 --> 00:51.680
along with crew members from JAXA and Roscosmos, will fly NASA's SpaceX crew 14 mission to the ISS,

00:51.680 --> 00:54.320
targeting Spring 2027 for launch.

00:54.320 --> 00:58.640
Their assignment keeps NASA's crew station cadence moving as the agency continues its

00:58.640 --> 01:02.080
long-duration science and operations aboard the Orbiting Laboratory.

01:03.040 --> 01:07.120
At the same time, NASA is also moving forward on another path to orbit.

01:07.120 --> 01:10.400
This week, NASA and Boeing shared updated Starliner plans,

01:10.400 --> 01:14.480
beginning with an uncrewed Starliner 1 mission to the International Space Station

01:14.480 --> 01:18.800
as early as December to validate spacecraft improvements before future crewed flights.

01:19.520 --> 01:24.000
NASA also announced astronaut Woody Hoberg as commander of the next crewed Starliner mission.

01:24.320 --> 01:27.280
Another step toward maintaining multiple American spacecraft for

01:27.280 --> 01:29.680
carrying astronauts to and from low-Earth orbit.

01:30.400 --> 01:33.920
Artemis III's SLS rocket just hit another major milestone.

01:34.480 --> 01:38.960
This week, teams at Kennedy Space Center completed stacking the final booster motor segments

01:38.960 --> 01:43.040
and installed all four RS-25 engines on the rocket's core stage.

01:43.040 --> 01:46.720
The next big step is integrating the core stage with the boosters in the coming weeks

01:46.720 --> 01:49.760
as we build towards this exciting mission in mid-2027.

01:50.320 --> 01:53.840
And finally, NASA's X-59 had a record-setting week.

01:53.840 --> 01:57.200
The quiet supersonic aircraft flew six times over three days

01:57.200 --> 02:03.040
and reached its fastest flight yet, clocking in at Mach 1.53, just over a thousand miles an hour.

02:03.680 --> 02:08.080
For work continues in orbit on the path to the moon and across the skies of the future.

02:08.080 --> 02:15.360
We'll see you next time on the NASA Minute.

